A||||4||84sleepr||timing gears?||||5||
Z||000000||84sleepr||11-16-2001||07:28 PM||||Just a quick question,<P>I'm replacing the timing cover gasket and seal cus they're leaking.  I figured that I'd replace the gears while i'm in there.<P>I have a 1984 2.5L, the question is.  I ordered the steel gears and they sent me one steel and one fiber kind.  I asked them if this was correct and they say yes.  I'm worried that the steel gear will just eat through the fiber one.  Just a quick question to avoid a possible problem...<P>Did they send me the correct set or did they mix them up, they come in separate boxes???<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||11-16-2001||07:50 PM||||They sent you the stock factory setup.  Unfortunatly, the fiber cam gear does tend to wear out and shed some teeth over time.  The cam gear can be a pain to change, especially with the engine in the car, since it is pressed onto the camshaft.  It can be done in the car, but it involves drilling and tapping the gear and cam to allow the use of a puller to get it off and a bolt and washer press to get it back on.  A lot of folks (me included when I had my 84) installed a set of all steel gears.  They are available at some parts stores.  I noticed no increase in noise and it should last nearly forever.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||84sleepr||11-17-2001||09:35 AM||||Thanks,<P>I'm going to try and get the all steel gears this morning.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||11-17-2001||12:35 PM||||How long do the stock ones that orignally come with the engine last? I remember hearing 130K miles somewhere. If a car is driven hard does this take away life from them?<P>Thanks<P>[edited for typos]<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||11-17-2001||08:01 PM||||With rare exceptions, the fiber gears last at least 100,000. As I remember, it's actaully made from a very tough material in the "phenolic" family of composites. Phenolics are probably the oldest and most understood of all composites out there. <P>A goof thing about these motors is that you don't whack a valve if the gear quits. Break a timing set on a Japanese motor and see what kind of mess you get.<P>Figure the one in there now is pushing 20 years old in a hurry.... It's more likely you'll need a full rebuild of the motor before second fiber set goes bad.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
